1/r_j+1 intervals of length at most r_j. Their products with the jth cover set cover its product with [0,1], and each has Euclidean diameter at most √2 r_j. The total (q+1)-cost is at most 2(√2)^(q+1)Σ_j r_j^q, and the cover diameters are at most √2δ. Letting the total q-cost tend to zero gives H^(q+1)(F)=0. Hence dim_H(F)≤q+1 for every q>D, and dim_H(F)≤D+1.\\\\n\\\\nSuppose D>0 and fix 0<s<D. Then H^s(E)=∞, so c_s(E,δ) tends to infinity as δ decreases to zero. Fix 0<δ≤1 and any countable cover (V_i) of F with ρ_i=diam(V_i)≤δ; discard empty members. Let A_i and B_i be its coordinate projections. Their diameters are at most ρ_i. For each i choose an open interval J_i containing B_i, of length at most ρ_i+η_i, where η_i>0. Choose the errors with Σ_i η_iρ_i^s<ε, for example η_i=ε2^(-i)/(1+ρ_i^s) after enumeration. For each t∈[0,1], the A_i with t∈B_i cover E: for each x∈E, (x,t) belongs to some V_i. Since B_i⊆J_i, the A_i with t∈J_i also cover E. Therefore c_s(E,δ)≤Σ_i 1_{J_i}(t)ρ_i^s for every t∈[0,1]. The right side is a measurable nonnegative series. Integrating and applying Tonelli gives c_s(E,δ)≤Σ_i |J_i∩[0,1]|ρ_i^s≤Σ_i(ρ_i+η_i)ρ_i^s≤Σ_iρ_i^(s+1)+ε. Zero-diameter terms contribute zero because s>0. Let ε tend to zero and then take the infimum over all product covers of scale δ; this gives c_s(E,δ)≤c_(s+1)(F,δ). Since c_s(E,δ) tends to infinity as δ decreases to zero, so does c_(s+1)(F,δ). Thus H^(s+1)(F)=∞ and dim_H(F)≥s+1. This holds for all 0<s<D, so dim_H(F)≥D+1.\\\\n\\\\nIf D=0, choose x₀∈E. The coordinate projection F→[0,1] is 1-Lipschitz and onto, since (x₀,t)∈F for every t. The standard Lipschitz dimension inequality and dim_H([0,1])=1 give dim_H(F)≥1=D+1, including n=0. Combined with the upper bound this proves the equality.\\\",\\\"unresolved_points\\\":[\\\"For a fully sourced proof or NL0, verify the pinned signatures and applicability of the threshold facts H^q(E)=0 for q>dim_H(E), and H^s(E)=∞ for 0<s<dim_H(E); the content-limit consequence follows directly from the Hausdorff-measure definition.\\\",\\\"For D=0, verify the standard Lipschitz nonincrease theorem for Hausdorff dimension and dim_H([0,1])=1 with exact source statements.\\\",\\\"The upper-bound argument uses D<∞, which follows from the finite-dimensional Euclidean ambient dimension bound; verify its exact source/application in a fully sourced proof.\\\",\\\"Tonelli for the nonnegative measurable indicator series is the only integration theorem needed in the slicing passage; no measurability of B_i is assumed.\\\"]}\"}","status":"completed"}}

